From 8c0705d656251162b2617e3775ffb3f39f17ce83 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: reaper <theunbounddeveloper@outlook.com>
Date: Sun, 16 Aug 2026 21:37:17 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] reaper_diag: add firewall/accelerator triage, and verify the
 sanitizer's output

A connectivity field report could not be answered from a diag bundle: the
collector captured no iptables output and no accelerator surfaces at all, so the
commands that separate "Warden dropped it" from "the accelerator wedged" from
"look upstream" could only be run by hand, live, on the box during an outage.
That is why that class of report stalls.

New sections (renumbered; nothing referenced the old numbers - checked both
Reaper_Diag.asp and do_reaper_diag_cgi in web.c):

  14. FIREWALL / WARDEN - rwarden_* config, packet/byte counters for all six
      Warden chains, top-level chain policies, ipset SIZES, live membership
      tests for three well-known resolvers, and drop-log volume by prefix.
      A climbing RW_ODROP or an "IN SET" hit is the answer outright.
  15. DATAPLANE ACCELERATOR - one-shot reads of /proc/gdx/skb_idx, /proc/gdx/
      stats, /proc/fpm/status, /proc/bpm/status.
  16. CONNECTIVITY PROBES - bounded pings to the default gateway and two named
      public resolvers.
  17. RWATCH - rwatch_* state and any /jffs/rwatch capture bundles.
  18/19. the former kernel log and syslog tails, renumbered.

SANITIZATION. One real gap closed and one guarantee added:

  - e-mail addresses were passing through. The nvram classifier only catches
    values whose KEY looks sensitive, so an address in a log line, a DDNS record
    or an addon's output was never redacted. New [EMAIL-n] pass, ordered before
    the MAC/IPv6/IPv4 passes so an addr@1.2.3.4 form is taken as one unit.
  - POST-SANITIZE TRIPWIRE: the finished body is re-scanned for public-IPv4 /
    MAC / e-mail shapes and the ledger prints CLEAN or REVIEW BEFORE SHARING.
    COUNTS ONLY - printing an offending value would put the leak into the very
    file being audited. It reuses the sanitizer's own privip() rules so the
    private addresses kept on purpose are not counted as residue. This is what
    stops "sanitized" degrading silently as sections are added later.

Two rules are written into the header for whoever adds the next section: never
emit a container's CONTENTS when its SIZE answers the question (ipset uses
`list -t`, header-only - the members are the user's own address data), and never
print a literal public address as a label (the sanitizer rewrites it to
[EXT-IP-n] and the line stops meaning anything; print the target's NAME).

BOUNDED ON PURPOSE. httpd is single-threaded and do_reaper_diag_cgi() popen()s
this script, so the GUI is blocked for the whole run. ping is -c 3 -W 1, the
/proc reads are head-limited, worst case is a few seconds inside the 180 s
lockfile. Do not add an unbounded command here.

The /proc/gdx reads are ONE-SHOT on operator demand and must never become a
poller: rwatch's equivalent check is opt-in and default OFF because a periodic
kernel-facing /proc reader produced two field reboot loops.

Verified: busybox ash -n (the router's shell), dash and sh all parse; against
crafted hostile input all 9 sensitive items are redacted - including a colonless
MAC and both e-mail forms - while the 5 diagnostic-payload items (private IP,
netmask, loopback, firmware version, ipset name) survive; the tripwire reads
0/0/0 on a clean body and fires on a dirty one.

Reaper_Diag.asp's ledger preview is extended to match. It stays literal English
by design - the 2026-08-15 i18n audit recorded it as mirroring the .txt
artifact, so no dict keys are needed.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 release/src/router/others/reaper_diag  | 202 ++++++++++++++++++++++++-
 release/src/router/www/Reaper_Diag.asp |  11 +-
 2 files changed, 208 insertions(+), 5 deletions(-)
 mode change 100755 => 100644 release/src/router/others/reaper_diag

diff --git a/release/src/router/others/reaper_diag b/release/src/router/others/reaper_diag
old mode 100755
new mode 100644
index d606ea820c..26d3ab1c0d
--- a/release/src/router/others/reaper_diag
+++ b/release/src/router/others/reaper_diag
@@ -11,13 +11,33 @@
 #    - usernames, DDNS/host names, SSID values (consistent [USER-n]/[SSID-n])
 #    - any MAC address, router or client   (consistent MAC-n pseudonyms)
 #    - any public IPv4 / any IPv6 address  (consistent [EXT-IP-n]/[IP6-n])
+#    - e-mail addresses                    (consistent [EMAIL-n])
 #  RFC1918/loopback/link-local IPv4 and channel/RF/QoS numbers are kept -
 #  they are the diagnostic payload and identify nothing.
 #  Pseudonyms are stable WITHIN one report (cross-referencing works) and
 #  meaningless across reports.
+#
+#  v1.2.0 adds a POST-SANITIZE TRIPWIRE: after the sanitizer runs, the finished
+#  body is re-scanned for anything still matching a public IPv4 / MAC / e-mail
+#  shape and the COUNTS (never the values) are printed in the ledger. A clean
+#  report says CLEAN; anything else says REVIEW BEFORE SHARING. This is what
+#  keeps "sanitized" from silently degrading as new sections are added.
+#
+#  TWO RULES FOR ANYONE ADDING A SECTION BELOW:
+#    1. Never emit a container's CONTENTS when its SIZE answers the question.
+#       ipset member lists are dumped with `ipset list -t` (header only) for
+#       exactly this reason - the members are the user's own address data.
+#    2. Never print a literal public address as a label. The sanitizer will
+#       rewrite it to [EXT-IP-n] and the line loses its meaning; print the
+#       NAME of the target instead ("Google-DNS"), as section 16 does.
+#
+#  COST NOTE: httpd is single-threaded and do_reaper_diag_cgi() popen()s this
+#  script, so the whole GUI is blocked while it runs. Everything added in
+#  v1.2.0 is bounded: the probes use short deadlines and the accelerator reads
+#  are one-shot cats. Do not add an unbounded command here.
 # ============================================================================
 PATH=/sbin:/bin:/usr/sbin:/usr/bin
-VER="REAPER-DIAG v1.0.1"
+VER="REAPER-DIAG v1.2.0"
 MODEL="$(nvram get productid 2>/dev/null)"
 EXTNO="$(nvram get extendno 2>/dev/null)"
 STAMP="$(date +%Y%m%d_%H%M%S)"
@@ -34,6 +54,35 @@ sec() { echo ""; echo "#### $1 ####"; }
 have() { type "$1" >/dev/null 2>&1; }  # v1.0.1: on-router busybox ash lacks "command -v"
 setchk() { v="$(nvram get "$1" 2>/dev/null)"; [ -n "$v" ] && echo "  $1 : SET (redacted)" || echo "  $1 : EMPTY"; }
 
+# v1.2.0 helpers. Both take the target's NAME and its address separately and
+# print only the NAME: a literal public address in the output would be rewritten
+# to [EXT-IP-n] by the sanitizer and the line would stop meaning anything.
+
+# "Is this destination currently inside a Warden set?" - membership, not contents.
+# This is the single test that separates a Warden drop from an upstream fault,
+# and it is why a report can now answer that without the operator running
+# `ipset test` by hand during an outage.
+wprobe() {
+  _l="$1"; _a="$2"; _hit=""
+  have ipset || { echo "  $_l : ipset absent"; return; }
+  for _s in rw_threat rw_ban $(ipset list -n 2>/dev/null | grep -E '^rw_g_'); do
+    ipset test "$_s" "$_a" >/dev/null 2>&1 && _hit="$_hit $_s"
+  done
+  [ -n "$_hit" ] && echo "  $_l : IN SET ->$_hit" || echo "  $_l : not in any Warden set"
+}
+
+# Bounded reachability probe. httpd is single-threaded and blocked for the whole
+# run, so the packet count and per-packet timeout are deliberately small; worst
+# case is ~3 s per target and typical is well under 1 s.
+probe() {
+  _l="$1"; _a="$2"
+  have ping || { echo "  $_l : ping absent"; return; }
+  _o="$(ping -c 3 -W 1 "$_a" 2>/dev/null | tr '\n' ' ')"
+  _loss="$(echo "$_o" | sed -n 's/.*[^0-9]\([0-9][0-9]*\)% packet loss.*/\1/p')"
+  _rtt="$(echo "$_o" | sed -n 's|.*= [0-9.]*/\([0-9.]*\)/.*|\1|p')"
+  echo "  $_l : loss=${_loss:-100}%  avg_rtt=${_rtt:-n/a}ms"
+}
+
 # ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
 # 1. Collect sensitive literals -> $SLIST as "CLASS<TAB>value" (never printed)
 # ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
@@ -71,7 +120,7 @@ BEGIN {
     t = index(l, "\t"); if (t < 2) continue
     n++; CLS[n] = substr(l, 1, t-1); VAL[n] = substr(l, t+1)
   }
-  cS=0; cU=0; cW=0; cH=0; cM=0; c6=0; c4=0
+  cS=0; cU=0; cW=0; cH=0; cM=0; c6=0; c4=0; cE=0
 }
 function toklit(cls, v) {
   if (v in LMAP) return LMAP[v]
@@ -100,6 +149,19 @@ function privip(ip,  a) {
     while ((p = index(line, v)) > 0)
       line = substr(line, 1, p-1) toklit(CLS[i], v) substr(line, p + length(v))
   }
+  # pass 1b (v1.2.0): e-mail addresses -> [EMAIL-n]. The nvram key classifier
+  # above only catches values whose KEY looks sensitive; an address that turns
+  # up in a log line, a DDNS record or an addon's output was passing straight
+  # through. Runs before the MAC/IPv6/IPv4 passes so an addr@1.2.3.4 form is
+  # taken as one unit rather than half-tokenized.
+  out = ""
+  while (match(line, /[A-Za-z0-9._%+-]+@[A-Za-z0-9.-]+\.[A-Za-z][A-Za-z]+/)) {
+    m = substr(line, RSTART, RLENGTH)
+    if (!(m in EMAP)) { cE++; EMAP[m] = "[EMAIL-" cE "]" }
+    out = out substr(line, 1, RSTART-1) EMAP[m]
+    line = substr(line, RSTART + RLENGTH)
+  }
+  line = out line
   # pass 2: MAC addresses -> MAC-n
   out = ""
   while (match(line, /[0-9a-fA-F][0-9a-fA-F]:[0-9a-fA-F][0-9a-fA-F]:[0-9a-fA-F][0-9a-fA-F]:[0-9a-fA-F][0-9a-fA-F]:[0-9a-fA-F][0-9a-fA-F]:[0-9a-fA-F][0-9a-fA-F]/)) {
@@ -154,6 +216,7 @@ END {
   print "  MAC addresses pseudonymized: " cM   > "/dev/stderr"
   print "  IPv6 addresses redacted    : " c6   > "/dev/stderr"
   print "  public IPv4 redacted       : " c4   > "/dev/stderr"
+  print "  e-mail addresses redacted  : " cE   > "/dev/stderr"
 }
 AWKEOF
 
@@ -324,10 +387,82 @@ echo "  jffs scripts: $(ls /jffs/scripts 2>/dev/null | tr '\n' ' ')"
 echo "  entware: $( [ -d /opt/bin ] && echo present || echo absent )"
 echo "  amtm-ish mounts: $(mount 2>/dev/null | grep -c /tmp/mnt)"
 
-sec "14. KERNEL LOG (tail)"
+sec "14. FIREWALL / WARDEN (counters and set SIZES - contents never dumped)"
+nvram show 2>/dev/null | grep '^rwarden_' | sort | sed 's/^/  /'
+echo "  -- chain packet/byte counters --"
+# The counters are the whole point: a climbing RW_ODROP during a reported
+# outage says Warden dropped it, and a flat one says look upstream. Before
+# v1.2.0 this could only be read by hand, live, on the box.
+for C in REAPER_WARDEN RW_OUT RW_SELF RW_DROP RW_ODROP RW_SDROP; do
+  if iptables -nvxL "$C" >/dev/null 2>&1; then
+    echo "  [$C]"; iptables -nvxL "$C" 2>/dev/null | sed 's/^/     /'
+  else
+    echo "  [$C] absent"
+  fi
+done
+echo "  -- top-level chains (policy + totals) --"
+for C in INPUT FORWARD OUTPUT; do
+  iptables -nvxL "$C" 2>/dev/null | head -2 | sed 's/^/     /'
+done
+echo "  -- ipset sizes: 'ipset list -t' is header-only, members are NEVER read --"
+if have ipset; then
+  for S in $(ipset list -n 2>/dev/null | grep '^rw_'); do
+    N="$(ipset list -t "$S" 2>/dev/null | awk -F': *' '/Number of entries/{print $2}')"
+    echo "     $S : ${N:-?} entries"
+  done
+else
+  echo "     ipset not present"
+fi
+echo "  -- is a well-known resolver currently inside a Warden set? --"
+wprobe "Cloudflare-DNS-v4 " 1.1.1.1
+wprobe "Google-DNS-v4     " 8.8.8.8
+wprobe "Quad9-DNS-v4      " 9.9.9.9
+echo "  -- Warden drop-log volume (counts only, no addresses) --"
+# grep -c prints 0 and exits 1 on no-match, but prints NOTHING on a missing
+# file - so capture first and default, rather than `|| echo 0` which would
+# emit a second 0 on the ordinary no-match case.
+for P in "REAPER-WARDEN " "REAPER-WARDEN-OUT " "REAPER-WARDEN-SELF "; do
+  WC="$(grep -c "$P" /tmp/syslog.log 2>/dev/null)"
+  echo "     '${P}' hits in syslog: ${WC:-0}"
+done
+
+sec "15. DATAPLANE ACCELERATOR (one-shot reads)"
+# ONE-SHOT, on operator demand only. rwatch's equivalent check is opt-in
+# (rwatch_gdx=1) and default OFF because a PERIODIC kernel-facing /proc reader
+# is what produced two field reboot loops. A single cat when someone asks for a
+# report is not that, and must never be turned into a poller here.
+for F in /proc/gdx/skb_idx /proc/gdx/stats /proc/fpm/status /proc/bpm/status; do
+  if [ -r "$F" ]; then
+    echo "  -- $F --"; head -16 "$F" 2>/dev/null | sed 's/^/     /'
+  else
+    echo "  $F : not present/readable"
+  fi
+done
+
+sec "16. CONNECTIVITY PROBES (targets named, addresses never printed)"
+# Separates "the router cannot reach the internet" from "a LAN client cannot",
+# and both from a Warden drop - read together with section 14.
+# These probes originate ON the router, so with rwarden_self=1 they traverse
+# RW_SELF and CAN be dropped by Warden itself. That is deliberate: a probe
+# showing 100% loss next to a section-14 "IN SET" hit for the same target is
+# the diagnosis, not a broken probe.
+GW="$(ip route 2>/dev/null | awk '/^default/{print $3; exit}')"
+if [ -n "$GW" ]; then probe "default gateway   " "$GW"; else echo "  default gateway   : NONE in routing table"; fi
+probe "Cloudflare-DNS-v4 " 1.1.1.1
+probe "Google-DNS-v4     " 8.8.8.8
+
+sec "17. RWATCH (self-monitor state)"
+nvram show 2>/dev/null | grep '^rwatch_' | sort | sed 's/^/  /'
+if [ -d /jffs/rwatch ]; then
+  echo "  -- captured failure bundles --"; ls -l /jffs/rwatch 2>/dev/null | sed 's/^/     /'
+else
+  echo "  /jffs/rwatch : none"
+fi
+
+sec "18. KERNEL LOG (tail)"
 dmesg 2>/dev/null | tail -120 | sed 's/^/  /'
 
-sec "15. SYSLOG (tail)"
+sec "19. SYSLOG (tail)"
 tail -150 /tmp/syslog.log 2>/dev/null | sed 's/^/  /'
 
 echo ""; echo "#### END OF DIAG ####"
@@ -338,15 +473,74 @@ echo ""; echo "#### END OF DIAG ####"
 # ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
 awk -v SFILE="$SLIST.s" -v FWVER="$(nvram get firmver 2>/dev/null)" -f "$AWKP" "$RAW" > "$BODY" 2> "$LEDG"
 
+# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
+# 4b. POST-SANITIZE TRIPWIRE (v1.2.0)
+# Re-scan the FINISHED body for anything still shaped like a public IPv4, a MAC
+# or an e-mail. This is not a second sanitizer - it is the check that the first
+# one actually worked, so that adding a section can never quietly start leaking.
+# It prints COUNTS ONLY; printing an offending value would put the leak into the
+# very file being audited. Uses the same privip() rules as the sanitizer so the
+# private addresses that are kept on purpose are not counted as residue.
+# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
+RESID="$(awk -v FWVER="$(nvram get firmver 2>/dev/null)" '
+  function privip(ip,  a) {
+    if (ip == FWVER) return 1
+    split(ip, a, ".")
+    if (a[1]>255||a[2]>255||a[3]>255||a[4]>255) return 1
+    if (a[1]==10 || a[1]==127 || a[1]==0) return 1
+    if (a[1]==192 && a[2]==168) return 1
+    if (a[1]==172 && a[2]>=16 && a[2]<=31) return 1
+    if (a[1]==169 && a[2]==254) return 1
+    if (a[1]>=224) return 1
+    return 0
+  }
+  {
+    line = $0
+    while (match(line, /[0-9][0-9]*\.[0-9][0-9]*\.[0-9][0-9]*\.[0-9][0-9]*/)) {
+      m = substr(line, RSTART, RLENGTH); if (!privip(m)) r4++
+      line = substr(line, RSTART + RLENGTH)
+    }
+    line = $0
+    while (match(line, /[0-9a-fA-F][0-9a-fA-F]:[0-9a-fA-F][0-9a-fA-F]:[0-9a-fA-F][0-9a-fA-F]:[0-9a-fA-F][0-9a-fA-F]:[0-9a-fA-F][0-9a-fA-F]:[0-9a-fA-F][0-9a-fA-F]/)) {
+      rM++; line = substr(line, RSTART + RLENGTH)
+    }
+    line = $0
+    while (match(line, /[A-Za-z0-9._%+-]+@[A-Za-z0-9.-]+\.[A-Za-z][A-Za-z]+/)) {
+      rE++; line = substr(line, RSTART + RLENGTH)
+    }
+  }
+  END { printf "%d %d %d", r4+0, rM+0, rE+0 }' "$BODY")"
+# shellcheck disable=SC2086 - deliberate word splitting of the "n n n" triple
+set -- $RESID
+R4="${1:-0}"; RM="${2:-0}"; RE="${3:-0}"
+if [ "$R4" = "0" ] && [ "$RM" = "0" ] && [ "$RE" = "0" ]; then
+  VERDICT="CLEAN - no public IPv4, MAC or e-mail shape survived the sanitizer"
+else
+  VERDICT="REVIEW BEFORE SHARING - residue detected, see counts above"
+fi
+
 {
   echo "==================== $MODEL Reaper sanitized diag ===================="
   echo " $VER | firmware: $EXTNO | generated: $(date)"
   echo ""
   echo "#### 0. SANITIZATION LEDGER (what was found and withheld) ####"
   cat "$LEDG"
+  echo ""
+  echo "  -- post-sanitize verification (counts only; values never printed) --"
+  echo "  residual public IPv4 shapes : $R4"
+  echo "  residual MAC shapes         : $RM"
+  echo "  residual e-mail shapes      : $RE"
+  echo "  VERDICT: $VERDICT"
+  echo ""
   echo "  NOTE: pseudonyms (MAC-n / [EXT-IP-n] / [SSID-n] ...) are consistent"
   echo "  within this report only. No credentials, MACs, host/user names or"
   echo "  external IPs are contained in this file. Safe to post publicly."
+  echo "  Set MEMBERS are never read - section 14 reports set SIZES only."
+  echo ""
+  echo "  Investigating a connectivity complaint? Read 14 (Warden counters +"
+  echo "  set membership) with 16 (probes): a climbing RW_ODROP or an IN SET"
+  echo "  hit means the router dropped it; flat counters with failing probes"
+  echo "  means look upstream."
   cat "$BODY"
   echo "==================== end diag $(date) ===================="
 } > "$OUT"
diff --git a/release/src/router/www/Reaper_Diag.asp b/release/src/router/www/Reaper_Diag.asp
index e5e20dd8c4..57717e5b3f 100644
--- a/release/src/router/www/Reaper_Diag.asp
+++ b/release/src/router/www/Reaper_Diag.asp
@@ -117,8 +117,17 @@ pre.sample .g{color:var(--jade)} pre.sample .r{color:#e0837d} pre.sample .w{colo
   SSID values redacted       : <span class="g">3</span>
   MAC addresses pseudonymized: <span class="g">14</span>
   public IPv4 redacted       : <span class="g">3</span>
+  e-mail addresses redacted  : <span class="g">0</span>
+
+  -- post-sanitize verification (counts only; values never printed) --
+  residual public IPv4 shapes : <span class="g">0</span>
+  residual MAC shapes         : <span class="g">0</span>
+  residual e-mail shapes      : <span class="g">0</span>
+  VERDICT: <span class="g">CLEAN</span> - no public IPv4, MAC or e-mail shape
+           survived the sanitizer
   <span class="r">NOTE: no credentials, MACs, host/user names or external IPs
-  are contained in this file. Safe to post publicly.</span></pre>
+  are contained in this file. Safe to post publicly.
+  Set MEMBERS are never read - firewall sizes only.</span></pre>
   </div>
   <div class="lg-r">
     <button class="btn" id="dl" type="button"><#RDIAG_23#></button>
